  "correctValue": null,
  "solution": {
    "text": "<blockquote><p>The potential is&nbsp;constant inside the sphere as the electric field is zero inside the sphere. So, the value is given by&nbsp;</p><p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>V</mi><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mi>Q</mi><mrow><mn>4</mn><mi>&#960;</mi><msub><mi>&#949;</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mi>R</mi></mrow></mfrac></math>&nbsp;for&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>r</mi><mo>&#60;</mo><mi>R</mi></math></p><p>The potential at the surface is&nbsp;</p><p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>V</mi><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mi>Q</mi><mrow><mn>4</mn><mi>&#960;</mi><msub><mi>&#949;</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mi>R</mi></mrow></mfrac></math></p><p>The potential outside the sphere starts decreasing and is given by the formula&nbsp;</p><p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>V</mi><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mi>Q</mi><mrow><mn>4</mn><mi>&#960;</mi><msub><mi>&#949;</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mi>r</mi></mrow></mfrac></math>&nbsp;for&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>r</mi><mo>&#62;</mo><mi>R</mi></math></p></blockquote>",
